      text: "The final semester seemed to move faster than anyone expected.\n\nEvery week brought new deadlines.\n\nNew assignments.\n\nNew presentations.\n\nNew reminders that university life was slowly coming to an end.\n\nStudents who once worried about exams were now worrying about something much larger.\n\nThe future.\n\nQuestions followed them everywhere.\n\nWhat happens after graduation?\n\nWill there be jobs?\n\nWill there be opportunities?\n\nWill the years spent studying actually lead somewhere?\n\nFor Brian, those questions never disappeared.\n\nIf anything, they grew louder.\n\nYet despite the pressure, another part of his life had quietly begun to change.\n\nHis friendship with Diana.\n\nWhat had started as occasional conversations after rehearsals had grown into something more familiar.\n\nNot romance.\n\nNot yet.\n\nJust a friendship that felt natural.\n\nComfortable.\n\nEasy.\n\nSome friendships required effort.\n\nTheirs did not.\n\nOne afternoon, after a lecture, Brian received a text message.\n\n**How was your presentation?**\n\nHe looked at the screen.\n\nIt was Diana.\n\nHe smiled.\n\n**Better than I expected.**\n\nA reply arrived almost immediately.\n\n**That's what everyone says after surviving one.**\n\nBrian laughed quietly.\n\nFrom that day, small conversations became more common.\n\nSometimes they exchanged messages about classes.\n\nSometimes about lecturers.\n\nSometimes about nothing important at all.\n\nDays would pass without communication.\n\nThen one of them would reach out.\n\nThe friendship never felt forced.\n\nIt simply existed.\n\nGrowing quietly in the background of their lives.\n\nOne evening, Brian sat in the library reviewing notes when his phone vibrated.\n\n**Still studying?**\n\nHe smiled.\n\n**Of course.**\n\n**You need help.**\n\n**I am helping myself.**\n\n**Normal people rest sometimes.**\n\n**Normal people also fail exams.**\n\nA few moments later, another message appeared.\n\n**You are impossible.**\n\nBrian laughed.\n\nStudents nearby looked at him curiously.\n\nHe quickly returned his attention to his books.\n\nYet the smile remained.\n\nOver time, Diana became one of the few people who could pull him away from his serious thoughts.\n\nShe challenged him.\n\nQuestioned him.\n\nSometimes teased him.\n\nAnd somehow, he never seemed to mind.\n\nOne Saturday afternoon, several students gathered at a café near campus.\n\nThe group included Brian, Diana, Kevin, and a few others.\n\nThe conversation drifted from one topic to another.\n\nPolitics.\n\nMusic.\n\nRelationships.\n\nFuture careers.\n\nAt one point, the discussion turned toward marriage.\n\n\"I want to get married before thirty,\" one student announced confidently.\n\nAnother laughed.\n\n\"You haven't even found a girlfriend.\"\n\n\"That is a minor detail.\"\n\nThe table erupted with laughter.\n\nSomeone turned toward Diana.\n\n\"What about you?\"\n\nShe thought for a moment.\n\n\"I want to marry someone I can trust.\"\n\nThe answer sounded simple.\n\nYet something about it felt sincere.\n\n\"What about you, Brian?\" Kevin asked.\n\nBrian shrugged.\n\n\"I haven't thought about it much.\"\n\nThe entire table groaned.\n\n\"That's a lie.\"\n\n\"It isn't.\"\n\n\"You think about everything.\"\n\n\"Not everything.\"\n\nDiana laughed.\n\n\"For once, I agree with Kevin.\"\n\nBrian shook his head.\n\nMarriage genuinely wasn't something he spent much time thinking about.\n\nHis focus remained fixed on graduation.\n\nBusiness.\n\nSuccess.\n\nBuilding a future.\n\nRelationships felt distant.\n\nLike something that belonged to another chapter of life.\n\nThe irony, of course, was that life rarely follows the plans people create for it.\n\nWeeks passed.\n\nThe friendship continued growing.\n\nBy now, Brian and Diana understood each other better.\n\nShe knew when he was stressed.\n\nHe knew when something was bothering her.\n\nThey had learned each other's habits.\n\nThe little things.\n\nThe things people only notice after spending time together.\n\nOne afternoon, they sat beneath their now familiar tree near the library.\n\nStudents moved around them as usual.\n\nThe campus buzzed with activity.\n\nYet their corner remained peaceful.\n\n\"What are you thinking about?\" Diana asked.\n\nBrian looked toward the distant buildings.\n\n\"The future.\"\n\nShe laughed immediately.\n\n\"I knew it.\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"You always think about the future.\"\n\n\"It matters.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nShe smiled.\n\n\"But sometimes I wonder if you're in such a hurry to reach tomorrow that you forget today.\"\n\nThe words lingered longer than she intended.\n\nBrian didn't answer immediately.\n\nPerhaps because part of him recognized the truth in them.\n\nFor years, he had measured life through goals.\n\nAchievements.\n\nMilestones.\n\nWhat came next.\n\nRarely what existed right now.\n\nEventually he smiled.\n\n\"Maybe.\"\n\nDiana smiled back.\n\nIt was one of those small conversations that seemed insignificant at the time.\n\nYears later, however, Brian would remember it clearly.\n\nVery clearly.\n\nAs graduation approached, reality slowly began pulling students in different directions.\n\nSome had already secured internships.\n\nOthers were applying for jobs.\n\nA few planned to return home after finishing their studies.\n\nNobody knew exactly what would happen next.\n\nWhat everyone understood was that life was changing.\n\nFast.\n\nOne evening, after a rehearsal for a final university event, Brian and Diana found themselves walking toward the main gate.\n\nThe sun was setting behind the campus buildings.\n\nThe sky glowed orange and gold.\n\nNeither seemed eager to end the conversation.\n\n\"You know,\" Diana said, \"we probably won't see each other much after graduation.\"\n\nThe statement caught Brian by surprise.\n\nHe had thought about graduation countless times.\n\nYet he had never considered that particular reality.\n\nPeople moved on.\n\nLives changed.\n\nFriendships faded.\n\nThat was simply how life worked.\n\n\"I suppose that's true,\" he said.\n\nFor a brief moment, neither spoke.\n\nThe silence felt different.\n\nNot uncomfortable.\n\nJust thoughtful.\n\nThen Diana smiled.\n\n\"Well, let's not disappear completely.\"\n\nBrian smiled back.\n\n\"We won't.\"\n\nIt sounded like a simple promise.\n\nOne of those things people say without thinking.\n\nYet both of them meant it.\n\nAt the gate, they said goodbye and went their separate ways.\n\nBrian watched her walk away before heading toward his room.\n\nFor some reason, the evening felt heavier than usual.\n\nNot sad.\n\nJust uncertain.\n\nThe future he had spent years planning was finally approaching.\n\nSoon university would end.\n\nThe routines would disappear.\n\nThe familiar faces would scatter across the country.\n\nAnd everyone would begin chasing the lives they had imagined.\n\nThat night, lying awake in bed, Brian stared at the ceiling.\n\nFor once, he wasn't thinking about business plans.\n\nOr graduation.\n\nOr success.\n\nInstead, he found himself wondering about something unexpected.\n\nWhether some people were meant to stay in your life.\n\nOr whether they were simply passing through.\n\nHe didn't know the answer.\n\nNot yet.\n\nBut one thing was becoming clear.\n\nDiana was no longer just another student on campus.\n\nShe was no longer simply the girl from the audience.\n\nSomewhere between conversations, shared laughter, and ordinary moments, she had become someone important.\n\nNeither of them realized it.\n\nBut the friendship they were building was about to face its first real test.\n\nBecause graduation was coming.\n\nAnd with it, the beginning of separate lives.\n"
