      text: "Seven Years Later\n\nNairobi had changed.\n\nNew buildings stretched higher into the skyline. Roads that once carried light traffic were now packed with vehicles from dawn until late evening. Entire neighborhoods had transformed. Restaurants had opened and closed. Businesses had risen and disappeared.\n\nSeven years was a long time.\n\nLong enough for dreams to either die or become reality.\n\nFor Brian, the years had been a mixture of both.\n\nHe stood near the window of his office on the fourth floor of a commercial building along Ngong Road, staring at the evening traffic below. The familiar sound of hooting vehicles drifted through the slightly opened window.\n\nHis office was not large, but it represented something far greater than its size.\n\nIt represented survival.\n\nThe journey after university had been far harder than he had imagined.\n\nThere had been months when business was so slow that he questioned every decision he had ever made.\n\nMonths when rent was due before clients paid.\n\nMonths when friends found stable jobs while he continued gambling on an uncertain future.\n\nBut he had refused to quit.\n\nAnd somehow, against the odds, things had begun to work.\n\nNot perfectly.\n\nNot dramatically.\n\nBut enough.\n\nEnough to move from surviving to growing.\n\nEnough to employ a few people.\n\nEnough to believe he was building something meaningful.\n\nThe office door opened.\n\n\"Boss, are you still here?\"\n\nBrian turned.\n\nIt was James, one of his employees.\n\n\"You people should go home,\" Brian said.\n\nJames laughed.\n\n\"You're the one still working.\"\n\n\"I'll leave shortly.\"\n\n\"You said that an hour ago.\"\n\nBrian smiled.\n\n\"Go before your wife starts blaming me.\"\n\nJames shook his head and left.\n\nA few moments later, Brian was alone again.\n\nThe silence reminded him of something.\n\nOr rather someone.\n\nDiana.\n\nThe thought appeared unexpectedly.\n\nIt happened from time to time.\n\nLess often than before.\n\nBut it still happened.\n\nOver the years, life had pulled them in different directions.\n\nAt first they communicated regularly.\n\nA phone call here.\n\nA message there.\n\nBirthday wishes.\n\nHoliday greetings.\n\nOccasional updates about life.\n\nThen careers became demanding.\n\nResponsibilities increased.\n\nDays became weeks.\n\nWeeks became months.\n\nThe conversations became fewer.\n\nEventually they almost disappeared.\n\nNot because of conflict.\n\nNot because of misunderstanding.\n\nSimply because life had happened.\n\nYet whenever Brian thought about university, one memory always appeared before the others.\n\nA blue notebook.\n\nA rainy afternoon outside a lecture hall.\n\nConversations that seemed ordinary at the time but somehow remained unforgettable.\n\nHe smiled slightly.\n\nFunny how some people never completely leave your story.\n\nEven when they are no longer part of your daily life.\n\nHis phone vibrated on the desk.\n\nA message from a client.\n\nBrian glanced at it briefly before switching off his computer.\n\nOutside, darkness had begun settling over Nairobi.\n\nBy the time he reached the parking area, the evening rush hour was in full force.\n\nMatatus blasted music.\n\nMotorcycles squeezed through impossible spaces.\n\nStreet vendors moved between vehicles selling snacks and bottled water.\n\nThe city was alive.\n\nAs always.\n\nThe drive home took nearly an hour.\n\nBy the time he entered his apartment, exhaustion had settled into his bones.\n\nHe dropped his keys on the table and loosened his tie.\n\nThe apartment was quiet.\n\nToo quiet.\n\nFor a moment he stood there without moving.\n\nThen his phone rang.\n\nAn unfamiliar number.\n\nBrian almost ignored it.\n\nAlmost.\n\nSomething made him answer.\n\n\"Hello?\"\n\nFor a second there was silence.\n\nThen a woman's voice spoke.\n\nA voice he had not heard in years.\n\n\"Brian?\"\n\nHe froze.\n\nHis heart skipped unexpectedly.\n\nThe voice sounded older.\n\nMore mature.\n\nYet instantly familiar.\n\n\"Diana?\"\n\nA soft laugh came through the phone.\n\n\"At least you still remember me.\"\n\nBrian sat down slowly.\n\nSeven years disappeared in an instant.\n\n\"How could I forget you?\"\n\nAnother brief silence followed.\n\nNot awkward.\n\nJust unexpected.\n\nTwo people standing on opposite sides of time trying to find their way back into a conversation that had paused years earlier.\n\n\"How have you been?\" Diana asked.\n\nBrian leaned back.\n\nA smile slowly appeared on his face.\n\nOutside, Nairobi continued moving through another ordinary evening.\n\nInside his apartment, something extraordinary had just begun.\n\nAnd neither of them knew that this single phone call would change the course of both their lives once again.\n",
