      text: "Six months later, Brian stood outside the house they had chosen as their first home.\n\nHe looked at the keys in his hand.\n\nThen at Diana.\n\nThen at Amara, who was running excitedly around the compound.\n\n\"Is this really ours?\" she asked.\n\nBrian laughed.\n\n\"Yes.\"\n\nShe ran toward him.\n\n\"We have a house!\"\n\nDiana laughed.\n\nBrian lifted her into his arms.\n\n\"Our house.\"\n\nAmara smiled.\n\nFor the first time, the word sounded complete.\n\nHome.\n\nMoving in was chaotic.\n\nBoxes were everywhere.\n\nFurniture arrived at different times.\n\nDiana spent hours deciding where everything should go.\n\nBrian tried to help.\n\nHe quickly discovered that helping and being told what to do were two very different things.\n\n\"Put that there.\"\n\n\"Where?\"\n\n\"There.\"\n\n\"Where is there?\"\n\nDiana pointed.\n\nBrian shook his head.\n\n\"Very clear.\"\n\nShe laughed.\n\nThe Amara watched them.\n\n\"Mommy always wins.\"\n\nBrian looked at her.\n\n\"Whose side are you on?\"\n\n\"Mommy's.\"\n\nDiana laughed.\n\nBrian pretended to be offended.\n\n\"Already?\"\n\nThe girl nodded seriously.\n\n\"Always.\"\n\nThe house filled with laughter.\n\nAnd for the first time, Brian realized how different life felt.\n\nHe had wanted success.\n\nHe had wanted money.\n\nHe had wanted to build something meaningful.\n\nBut he had never understood that a home could be measured by the sound of laughter.\n\nBrian became deeply involved in Amara's life.\n\nHe attended school meetings.\n\nHelped with homework.\n\nBought books.\n\nListened to stories that sometimes made absolutely no sense.\n\nShe would sit beside him while he worked and ask endless questions.\n\n\"What are you doing?\"\n\n\"Working.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because I have to.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Because that's what adults do.\"\n\n\"Why?\"\n\nBrian would stare at her.\n\nShe would laugh.\n\nDiana would hear them from another room and smile.\n\nOne evening, Diana walked into the living room and found Brian asleep on the sofa.\n\nAmara was sleeping beside him, her head resting against his arm.\n\nA book lay open on the floor.\n\nDiana stood there quietly.\n\nShe took a photograph.\n\nNot because the moment was perfect.\n\nBecause it was ordinary.\n\nAnd she knew ordinary moments were often the ones people remembered most.\n\nMonths passed.\n\nTheir home became warmer.\n\nPictures appeared on the walls.\n\nTheir wedding photograph occupied a place of honor.\n\nAmara 's drawings covered the refrigerator.\n\nLife was becoming comfortable.\n\nPerhaps too comfortable.\n\nOne evening, Diana stood in the bathroom staring at a pregnancy test.\n\nHer hands trembled.\n\nShe looked at it again.\n\nPositive.\n\nShe sat down.\n\nTears filled her eyes.\n\nNot fear.\n\nNot sadness.\n\nSomething deeper.\n\nShe laughed softly.\n\nThen covered her mouth.\n\n\"Brian.\"\n\nShe waited until he came home.\n\nHe walked through the door carrying his briefcase.\n\n\"Where's my favorite girl?\"\n\nAmara ran toward him.\n\n\"Daddy!\"\n\nHe lifted her.\n\nThen looked toward Diana.\n\nShe was standing in the doorway.\n\nSomething about her expression made him pause.\n\n\"What happened?\"\n\nDiana smiled.\n\n\"Put her down.\"\n\nBrian looked confused.\n\n\"Why?\"\n\n\"Just put her down.\"\n\nHe did.\n\nDiana walked toward him.\n\nShe held out the pregnancy test.\n\nBrian stared at it.\n\nFor several seconds, he said nothing.\n\nThen he looked at Diana.\n\n\"Is this...\"\n\nShe nodded.\n\n\"We're having a baby.\"\n\nBrian's eyes filled with tears.\n\nHe pulled her into his arms.\n\nAmara looked up at them.\n\n\"What's happening?\"\n\nBrian laughed through his tears.\n\n\"You're going to be a big sister.\"\n\nHer eyes became enormous.\n\n\"Really?\"\n\n\"Really.\"\n\nShe screamed with excitement.\n\nAnd suddenly the house wasn't just full of hopes.\n\nIt was full of another future.",
