      part: "PART II — THE BOUNDARY LINES",
      text: "The following morning, Ben arrived at the office feeling unusually lighter.\n\nNot because anything significant had happened.\n\nAt least that's what he told himself.\n\nHe had simply helped a colleague recover a report.\n\nA small act.\n\nA normal act.\n\nThe kind of thing he did all the time.\n\nYet as he settled into his chair and opened his inbox, he found himself replaying the conversation from the previous evening.\n\nThe laughter.\n\nThe jokes.\n\nThe ease with which they had spoken.\n\nStrange.\n\nFor weeks, Claire had existed only as an observation.\n\nNow she had become a person.\n\nAnd somehow that made all the difference.\n\nHis phone buzzed.\n\nA client.\n\nThen another.\n\nThen another.\n\nBy ten o'clock, Ben was buried beneath emails, deployment requests, and support tickets.\n\nWork had a way of silencing unnecessary thoughts.\n\nOr at least delaying them.\n\nAt eleven-thirty, he stepped out for coffee.\n\nAs he approached the kitchen area, he noticed Claire standing by the vending machine.\n\nThis time she saw him first.\n\n\"The computer hero.\"\n\nBen laughed.\n\n\"I saved one report.\"\n\n\"You saved two hours of my life.\"\n\n\"That's still just one report.\"\n\nShe shook her head.\n\n\"I disagree.\"\n\nThe conversation flowed naturally.\n\nEasier than either expected.\n\nNo awkward pauses.\n\nNo forced topics.\n\nJust two people talking.\n\nThe way people do when they genuinely enjoy each other's company.\n\n\"So,\" Claire said, stirring sugar into her tea, \"do you always stay that late?\"\n\n\"Usually.\"\n\n\"Voluntarily?\"\n\n\"Most days.\"\n\nShe stared at him.\n\n\"You actually enjoy working?\"\n\n\"I enjoy building things.\"\n\n\"That's not normal.\"\n\n\"I've been told.\"\n\nClaire laughed.\n\n\"Several times?\"\n\n\"More than several.\"\n\nThe smile remained on her face.\n\nFor a brief moment, neither spoke.\n\nThen Claire tilted her head slightly.\n\n\"So where do you live?\"\n\nThe question was simple.\n\nOrdinary.\n\nYet it felt different.\n\nMore personal than anything they had discussed before.\n\n\"Thika,\" Ben replied.\n\nHer eyebrows lifted.\n\n\"Seriously?\"\n\n\"Why do people always react like that?\"\n\n\"Because you commute every day.\"\n\n\"It's not that far.\"\n\n\"It is if Nairobi traffic decides it hates you.\"\n\n\"Traffic hates everyone.\"\n\n\"Fair point.\"\n\nBen took a sip of coffee.\n\n\"What about you?\"\n\n\"Nairobi.\"\n\n\"That narrows it down.\"\n\n\"I'm not giving my full address to a man I met beside a frozen computer.\"\n\nBen laughed.\n\n\"Smart.\"\n\n\"I know.\"\n\nThe conversation continued for several minutes before work pulled them back in different directions.\n\nYet throughout the afternoon, Ben noticed something had changed.\n\nNot dramatically.\n\nJust slightly.\n\nThey greeted each other now.\n\nStopped occasionally to talk.\n\nShared small observations about work.\n\nTiny interactions.\n\nThe kind that seem insignificant while they're happening.\n\nThe kind that quietly build familiarity.\n\nBy evening, the office was busy preparing for an upcoming internal event.\n\nPeople moved between departments carrying documents and supplies.\n\nMark appeared in Ben's office carrying a folder.\n\n\"You've been smiling a lot lately.\"\n\nBen didn't even look up.\n\n\"No, I haven't.\"\n\n\"You have.\"\n\n\"I haven't.\"\n\n\"You absolutely have.\"\n\nBen continued typing.\n\nMark leaned against the doorway.\n\n\"Coffee machine conversations?\"\n\nSilence.\n\n\"Aha.\"\n\n\"There is no aha.\"\n\n\"There definitely is.\"\n\nBen sighed.\n\n\"Don't you have work?\"\n\n\"I do.\"\n\n\"Then go do it.\"\n\nMark grinned.\n\n\"You know her name now.\"\n\nThe silence that followed answered the question.\n\nMark's grin widened.\n\n\"Oh, this is getting interesting.\"\n\n\"Nothing is getting interesting.\"\n\n\"That's exactly what people say right before things become interesting.\"\n\nBen pointed toward the corridor.\n\n\"Out.\"\n\nMark laughed and disappeared.\n\nThe office returned to normal.\n\nOr at least normal on the surface.\n\nBecause beneath the routine of meetings and deadlines, something new was forming.\n\nNot romance.\n\nNot yet.\n\nNot even attraction in the traditional sense.\n\nIt was simply curiosity evolving into connection.\n\nA stranger becoming familiar.\n\nA face becoming a person.\n\nA conversation becoming something worth continuing.\n\nAs Ben packed his laptop that evening, his phone vibrated.\n\nA message notification.\n\nNothing unusual.\n\nUntil he looked at the sender.\n\nFor a second, he simply stared.\n\nThen smiled.\n\nThe screen displayed a name he hadn't expected to see outside office hours.\n\nClaire.\n\nAnd just like that, the conversation was no longer confined to the corridors between their desks.",
      title: "Chapter 6 - Where Do You Live?",
      subtitle: "The moment people begin asking personal questions is often the moment a connection starts crossing invisible lines."
