      text: "Ben stared at the notification for several seconds before opening it.\n\nIt was a simple message.\n\nNothing dramatic.\n\nNothing flirtatious.\n\nNothing that would have seemed important to anyone else.\n\nClaire: Thanks again for yesterday 😊\n\nThat was it.\n\nOne sentence.\n\nOne emoji.\n\nYet somehow it felt different seeing her name appear on his phone outside office hours.\n\nBen smiled before typing a reply.\n\nBen: You're welcome. Did you remember to save your work today?\n\nThe response arrived almost immediately.\n\nClaire: Twice 😂\n\nBen: Progress.\n\nClaire: I learn from experience.\n\nThe conversation should have ended there.\n\nInstead, it continued.\n\nOne message became three.\n\nThree became ten.\n\nBefore either of them realized it, nearly an hour had passed.\n\nThe topics weren't particularly important.\n\nWork.\n\nTraffic.\n\nFavorite foods.\n\nFunny office stories.\n\nThe kind of conversation that develops naturally when two people enjoy talking to each other.\n\nWhen Ben finally put his phone down, it was almost midnight.\n\nFor the first time in a long while, he went to sleep smiling.\n\nOver the next few days, messaging became normal.\n\nNot constant.\n\nNot excessive.\n\nJust consistent.\n\nA funny observation during lunch.\n\nA joke after work.\n\nAn occasional question.\n\nThe rhythm felt effortless.\n\nAnd that was what made it dangerous.\n\nBecause the most powerful habits are rarely the ones we intentionally create.\n\nThey are the ones that quietly form without our permission.\n\nBy Friday, Ben found himself checking his phone more often than usual.\n\nNot obsessively.\n\nJust enough to notice.\n\nA message from Claire had somehow become one of the more pleasant parts of his day.\n\nSaturday morning arrived with a rare luxury.\n\nNo meetings.\n\nNo client deadlines.\n\nNo emergency support calls.\n\nBen spent the morning working on Haven Portal from home.\n\nThe system was progressing well.\n\nSeveral new features had been completed.\n\nA few bugs remained.\n\nNothing serious.\n\nAround noon, his phone buzzed.\n\nClaire.\n\nAgain, he smiled automatically.\n\nClaire: Working?\n\nBen: Always.\n\nClaire: That's unhealthy.\n\nBen: That's business.\n\nClaire: Same thing.\n\nBen laughed.\n\nA few more messages followed.\n\nThen more.\n\nThe conversation drifted effortlessly from topic to topic.\n\nAt some point Claire mentioned a restaurant she had visited recently.\n\nA small place with good food and terrible parking.\n\nThe discussion sparked another.\n\nThen another.\n\nThen another.\n\nWithout thinking too much about it, Ben typed a question.\n\nBen: So what's your favorite dinner place?\n\nThe typing indicator appeared.\n\nDisappeared.\n\nReturned.\n\nFinally her response arrived.\n\nClaire: Why? Planning to take me somewhere? 😄\n\nBen stared at the screen.\n\nFor a brief moment his heart skipped.\n\nNot because of the question itself.\n\nBecause of how easily it had appeared.\n\nPlayful.\n\nUnexpected.\n\nDangerously easy to misinterpret.\n\nHis analytical mind immediately started processing possibilities.\n\nWas she joking?\n\nWas she curious?\n\nWas she inviting something?\n\nWas she simply being friendly?\n\nHumans were exhausting.\n\nA server error was easier to decode.\n\nAfter several seconds he replied.\n\nBen: Maybe.\n\nThe typing indicator appeared almost instantly.\n\nThen disappeared.\n\nThen returned.\n\nFinally:\n\nClaire: Maybe? 😂\n\nBen: Depends on the answer.\n\nA longer pause followed.\n\nLong enough for Ben to wonder whether he had crossed a line.\n\nThen her reply arrived.\n\nClaire: Smooth.\n\nHe laughed.\n\nThe conversation continued.\n\nBut something had shifted.\n\nA subtle change in direction.\n\nNot enough to define.\n\nJust enough to notice.\n\nLater that evening, Mark called.\n\n\"Still alive?\"\n\n\"Barely.\"\n\n\"Working?\"\n\n\"Of course.\"\n\nMark sighed dramatically.\n\n\"You need help.\"\n\n\"Daniel already told me that.\"\n\n\"Daniel is right.\"\n\n\"That's concerning.\"\n\n\"It should be.\"\n\nA pause followed.\n\nThen Mark spoke again.\n\n\"So.\"\n\nBen immediately recognized the tone.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"I haven't asked anything.\"\n\n\"You don't need to.\"\n\nMark laughed.\n\n\"How's Claire?\"\n\nBen closed his eyes.\n\n\"I hate both of you.\"\n\n\"I'll take that as good.\"\n\n\"You know what?\"\n\n\"What?\"\n\n\"You spend too much time worrying about my life.\"\n\n\"Someone has to.\"\n\nThe conversation ended a few minutes later.\n\nBut the question lingered.\n\nHow's Claire?\n\nIt was a simple question.\n\nYet Ben realized he already knew the answer.\n\nGood.\n\nThe conversations were good.\n\nThe laughter was good.\n\nThe connection felt good.\n\nMaybe that was enough.\n\nMaybe he didn't need to define it.\n\nMaybe he could simply enjoy it for what it was.\n\nThe problem, however, was that human beings rarely leave good things undefined for very long.\n\nAnd somewhere ahead, waiting quietly beyond the comfort of messages and jokes, a question was approaching.\n\nA question neither of them was fully prepared to answer.",
