      text: "After the disappointment of the dead date, Ben expected to feel better.\n\nIn some ways, he did.\n\nThe realization had been useful.\n\nNecessary, even.\n\nHe now understood that the café situation had never been real.\n\nIt had been a possibility.\n\nA story.\n\nA future version of events that existed entirely inside his own imagination.\n\nKnowing that should have solved everything.\n\nIt didn't.\n\nBecause awareness and change are not the same thing.\n\nMonday morning arrived with its usual routines.\n\nTraffic.\n\nCoffee.\n\nEmails.\n\nMeetings.\n\nLife moved forward exactly as it always did.\n\nYet Ben noticed something.\n\nHe had become cautious.\n\nNot distant.\n\nNot cold.\n\nJust careful.\n\nThe kind of careful that appears after disappointment.\n\nThe kind of careful people mistake for wisdom.\n\nSometimes it is.\n\nSometimes it's just fear wearing a smarter outfit.\n\nAround ten-thirty, Claire stopped by his office.\n\n\"Morning.\"\n\n\"Morning.\"\n\nShe studied him briefly.\n\n\"You're quiet.\"\n\nBen smiled.\n\n\"I'm working.\"\n\n\"That's becoming your default answer.\"\n\n\"It's usually true.\"\n\nClaire laughed.\n\nThen sat down.\n\nFor the next several minutes they talked about work.\n\nClients.\n\nDeadlines.\n\nOffice gossip.\n\nNormal things.\n\nThe conversation felt exactly the same as always.\n\nYet Ben noticed himself watching for signals.\n\nAnalyzing reactions.\n\nMeasuring effort.\n\nSearching for certainty.\n\nAgain.\n\nThe realization annoyed him.\n\nBecause he thought he had learned the lesson already.\n\nApparently not.\n\nLater that afternoon, while reviewing a project timeline, Daniel called.\n\nThe timing was unfortunate.\n\nOr perhaps perfect.\n\nDepending on perspective.\n\nAfter listening to Ben describe the previous week, Daniel remained silent.\n\nThen asked:\n\n\"Can I tell you something you won't like?\"\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Good.\"\n\nDaniel smiled.\n\n\"You're building traps.\"\n\nBen frowned.\n\n\"What does that mean?\"\n\n\"You keep creating situations where you can get hurt.\"\n\nThe statement lingered.\n\nDaniel continued.\n\n\"You build expectations.\"\n\n\"You imagine outcomes.\"\n\n\"You search for signs.\"\n\n\"And then you react to things that haven't actually happened.\"\n\nBen leaned back.\n\nThe uncomfortable part wasn't that Daniel was speaking.\n\nThe uncomfortable part was that he was right.\n\nAgain.\n\n\"Do you know the difference between reality and fear?\" Daniel asked.\n\n\"No.\"\n\n\"Reality is what happened.\"\n\n\"And fear?\"\n\n\"What you think might happen.\"\n\nBen stared at the desk.\n\nBecause lately he had spent far too much time living inside the second category.\n\nThe rest of the afternoon passed slowly.\n\nWork helped.\n\nWork always helped.\n\nCode was predictable.\n\nSystems were logical.\n\nDatabases followed rules.\n\nHuman beings didn't.\n\nEspecially not Claire.\n\nNear the end of the day, Ben received a message.\n\nClaire: Quick question.\n\nBen: Dangerous start.\n\nClaire: Relax 😂\n\nBen: Never.\n\nClaire: I need help with something tomorrow.\n\nBen smiled.\n\nA week earlier, that message would have immediately triggered analysis.\n\nQuestions.\n\nPossibilities.\n\nExpectations.\n\nToday, however, he paused.\n\nThen simply replied.\n\nBen: Sure.\n\nNothing more.\n\nNo assumptions.\n\nNo predictions.\n\nJust an answer.\n\nThe simplicity felt surprisingly freeing.\n\nBecause for once, he wasn't trying to figure out what the request meant.\n\nHe wasn't searching for hidden messages.\n\nHe wasn't building a future from a single sentence.\n\nHe was responding to reality.\n\nNot imagination.\n\nThat night, while sitting on his balcony overlooking the city lights, Ben found himself reflecting.\n\nThe last few weeks had taught him something important.\n\nNot about Claire.\n\nAbout himself.\n\nAbout the stories he created.\n\nThe conclusions he rushed toward.\n\nThe expectations he quietly nurtured.\n\nThe fears he disguised as logic.\n\nThe hope he disguised as analysis.\n\nEvery trap had started the same way.\n\nA thought.\n\nA possibility.\n\nA question.\n\nThen a story.\n\nThen an emotional investment.\n\nLong before reality had provided any evidence.\n\nFor the first time, he saw the pattern clearly.\n\nAnd once you see a pattern, it becomes difficult to ignore.\n\nThe challenge wasn't understanding it.\n\nThe challenge was breaking it.\n\nBecause habits of the mind are harder to change than habits of the body.\n\nHis phone vibrated one final time before midnight.\n\nClaire.\n\nClaire: Goodnight, Ben.\n\nHe smiled.\n\nThen typed back.\n\nBen: Goodnight, Claire.\n\nSimple.\n\nOrdinary.\n\nNothing hidden.\n\nNothing imagined.\n\nJust a message.\n\nAnd perhaps that was enough.\n\nAt least for tonight.\n\nBecause sometimes the strongest thing a person can do is stop building stories and start living in reality.\n\nAnd Ben was finally beginning to learn the difference.",
